          (b) (i)             Some candidates failed to spell “venereal” correctly and some referred to sperms instead of
                              semen.  Some candidates also wrote “many cows can be fertilised” instead of many cows can
                              be inseminated at the same time.
                              Answer
                              No need to buy and care for the bull on the farm/cheaper than buying and caring for a bull;
                              Prevents the spread of sexually transmitted diseases;

                 Semen can be frozen and kept for many years/can be used even after the death of a donor bull;
                 Prevent big bulls from mating with small heifers, therefore, preventing injuries;
                 Semen from one bull can be used to inseminate many cows / high conception rate can be achieved;
      (ii) Most candidates knew how the process works, however, they referred to eggs being transferred
                 from    the    donor    to    the    surrogate    mother    instead    of    embryos.        Some    candidates    wrote    Follicle
                 Stimulating hormone as the one fertilising the egg instead of it stimulating multiple ovulation.
                 Answer
                 Synchronisation of oestrus in donor and recipient cows;
                 FSH given to donor cows to stimulate multiple ovulation/superovulation of donor cows;
                 Insemination of donor cows / fertilising the egg cells to form embryos;
                 Flushing of the embryo from the uterus;
                 Transfer embryo into the uterus of recipient cow;

     (c) (i)     The majority of candidates could not identify the type of cell division and some of the few
                 candidates who could identify it correctly failed to give a correct reason for their answer.
                 Answer
                 Meiosis; because genetic material is reduced into half/ diploid (2n) changed into haploid (n)/ reduction
                 division;
      (ii) Well answered.  Some candidates, however, failed to spell “spermatids” correctly and some
                 wrote spermatogenesis instead of spermatozoa.
                 Answer
                 C:  spermatids;
                 D:  spermatozoa/sperm cells;
      (iii) Poorly answered.  The majority of candidates could not name or describe the congenital defects
                 of testis that lead to infertility.  Very few candidates who could name the defects could not spell
                 cryptorchidism correctly.
                 Answer
                 Cryptorchidism is the condition whereby the testes remain in the abdominal cavity and do not descend
                 into the scrotum;
                 Hypoplasia is the condition whereby the testes are underdeveloped;

7 (a) The uses of a tractor on the farm are well known by most of the candidates.  Some candidates,
           however, provided answers such as “lifting heavy equipment and ploughing” which are incorrect.  It
           should also be noted that the tractor powers other machineries and not implements.
           Answer
           To pull implements and machineries;
           For powering machines;
           Personal transport around the farm;

     (b)   Well answered.  The majority of candidates could state the advantages of mechanisation in agriculture.
           Answer
           Increases speed and efficiency of labour/it is faster;
           Reduced labour costs;
           Reduced need for draft animals;
           Increased capacity to work larger farms/makes the work easier;
           Increased farm productivity and income;
           Reduced repetitive tasks;
           Reduced health hazards;
           Increased processing of produce;
           Availability of labour for other sectors;
           Improved quality of products;

     (c)   Most candidates scored one mark for this question.  The reasons suggested by some candidates
           were not explicit enough e.g. stay longer, last longer, reduces costs.
           Answer
           It keeps the tractor running well and for longer;
           Lubrication/greasing ensures there is less wear and tear/prevents wear and tear;
           It makes the tractor more efficient and cost effective;
           It ensures the tractor is safe to use/prevents hazards;
           It ensures compliance with the manufacturer ’s instructions, machine warranty and maintenance log;

