SECTION B

9 (a) Most candidates scored two marks as they could not explain the concept fully and some focused on
            sustainable agriculture instead of sustainable development
            Answer
            Sustainable development focuses on meeting the needs of the present generation; without compromising
            the ability of the future generation to meet their needs; by using natural resources wisely without damaging/
            depleting them;
            It comprises of three pillars: economic, environmental and social;

      (b)   Only a few candidates scored full marks for this question.  Some focused more on the impact of
            deforestation instead of conventional farming in general.
            Answer
            Mechanisation leading to soil compaction/ destruction of soil structure/water logging due to over irrigation;
            increase in the use of fossil fuels leading to pollution/air pollution;
            Water pollution by overuse of fertilisers;

           overuse of pesticides/herbicides pollute the soil/ kill beneficial organisms;
           soil erosion;
           soil salinization;

     (c)   Most candidates could give two to three correct answers to this question.  They focused more on
           subsidised inputs, e.g. seeds, fertilisers, start-up capital etc. which were considered as alternative
           answers, hence could not score more marks.
           Answer
           Increased ability to purchase commercial farms/access to subsidised/leasehold land;
           increased access to local market/ transport incentives to transport produce to the market;
           support of small-scale farmers in irrigation schemes;
           increased access to green jobs/farming enterprises;
           increased access to financial services;
           improved rural agricultural performance/increased production;
           Reduced livestock loses;
           engage youth in dialogues of policy making;
           provision of knowledge/training/information education;

     (d)   This question was well answered.  Some candidates, however, referred to chemical hazards and some
           were  giving  protective  measures  that  one  needs  when  working  with  machines  instead  of  describing
           the physical hazards.
           Answer
           Tools, implements and heavy machineries used on the farm may cause injuries due to sharp edges or
           machinery accidents;
           sharp-edged leaves, spines and thorns may cause injuries when farmers are handling them;
           carrying/ lifting heavy loads such as fertiliser bags may cause neck and back injuries;
           Inhaling dust may cause irritation and injuries to the lungs;
           Handling of animals may result in injuries due to kicking, biting;

     (e)   Most candidates could correctly discuss welfare support of farm workers by employers.  Some
           candidates, however, talked about safety precautions that need to be taken in the work environment.
           Answer
           Show compassion by addressing issues that create health and welfare problems for workers;
           Offer flexible working hours;
           Offer additional training on how to deal with colleagues with health problems;
           Regular health programmes at work to make people more aware of their own and co-workers’ health and of
           ways of dealing with health and welfare situations;

10 (a) Most candidates failed to mention “root development/growth” as the main function of phosphorus.
           Some candidates confused correcting deficiency with deficiency symptoms hence lost marks.
           Answer
           Promotes early root formation/stimulates root development;
           Growth of new tissues through cell division and enlargement/
           Promotes flowers, seeds and fruit formation/
           Controls the maturation of the crop/
           Needed for photosynthesis/ respiration/storage/transportation of nutrients/
           Needed for gene replication/
           Needed to survive in harsh winter conditions;
           Ways to correct deficiency
           Add Superphosphate;
           Add NPK fertiliser;
           Adjust the soil pH if it is too high or too low;
           Add manure/compost/organic matter;

     (b)   Most candidates could score a mark for “no need to buy the fertiliser”.
           Answer
      i. Cheap/no need to buy fertilisers;
      ii. Reduced pollution of air/carbon dioxide emission during burning of fossil fuels to produce
                 fertilisers/environmental friendly;
                 Reduced pollution of soil/water by nitrogenous compounds/eutrophication;
                 Symbiotic relationship of bacteria and leguminous plants;

     (c)   Well answered as most candidates could state the factors that influence soil colour.
           Answer
           Parental materials - the rocks from which the soil form;
           Soil water content - more water in the soil, darker than it usually is;
           Organic matter content - to organic matter that breaks down, the colour imparted to the soil varies
           from brown to black;
           Slope - the fact that at the top of the slope, the soil tends to be lighter in colour than at the bottom of a slope;

     (d)   The majority of candidates misinterpreted the question and discussed ways to prevent salinity in
           general instead of focusing on salinity due to irrigation and high evaporation.
           Answer
           optimise irrigation/do not over irrigate;
           use desalinated/recycled/rain-harvested water;
           add organic matter/manure to the soil; grow deep-rooted plants;
           avoid deep tillage/practise minimum tillage;
           use cover crops/mulch to reduce evaporation;

     (e)   Some candidates lost marks as they focused on the formation of Arenosols rather than its characteristics.
           Answer
           Arenosols high water permeability/fast drainage/low water-holding capacity/poor water retention;
           Vulnerable to wind erosion;
           low specific heat/fast heat absorption;
           low organic/humus content;
           minimal nutrient content/poor fertility;

11 (a) Most candidates could outline the ways but failed to discuss further as to how the environment will be
           affected.
           Answer
           Loss of biodiversity as species of wild plants and animals are removed and replaced by single types of
           crops;
           Damage to the soil as machinery causes soil compaction resulting in loss of productive land;
           Clearing of land exposes the soil to erosion;
           Air pollution as harmful gases are released into the atmosphere, contributing to climate change and
           increased rate of decomposition of organic matter in the soil, therefore, more carbon dioxide is produced;
           Soil and water pollution machinery allow farmers to apply large amounts of agricultural chemicals, which
           may enter the soil / or be washed into water sources causing pollution.

     (b)   Most candidates seemed familiar with maintenance of a planter.
           Answer
           empty and clean all hoppers/clean the planter thoroughly;
           Inspect the metering system/hoses and remove obstructions that might prevent the mechanisms from
           operating properly;
           Replace worn or broken parts, nuts and bolts;
           Prevent dirt from getting into the bearings and wipe off fittings;
           Use the correct type of lubricant to lubricate moving parts/keep all moving parts lubricated/lubricate all
           bearings;
           Paint any exposed metal surfaces;
           Cover all the furrow openers with protective coverings;
           Store the planter away from moisture when not in use;
           The planter should be stored inside a building with the wheels off the ground;

     (c)   Some candidates scored marks for this question, however, some lost marks for referring to the benefit
           of genetic engineering in crops though the question was specific to farm animals.
           Answer
           improved food/meat/milk quantity/increased production;
           produce disease resistant animals;
           improved growth rate/feed conversion efficiency;
           improved fertility;
           produce lean meat/low fat milk/improves the quality of products/improved taste;
           improve anti-microbial properties of milk for new born animals;
           improves animal health/sturdiness/hardiness/adaptability;

     (d)   Most candidates repeated the same role under universities and research institutes, hence, lost marks.
           Answer
           Produce qualified graduates who can work in the agricultural sector;
           Provide training to farmers and extension workers;
           Conduct scientific research on problems that affect farmers;
           Developing of improved varieties/animal medicines;

     (e)   This question was poorly answered as most candidates referred to challenges experienced in
           agricultural extension work instead of focussing on difficulties on attitude change.
           Answer
           Bad experience with previous extension activities;
           Traditional rural communities are also likely to be suspicious and prefer their traditional methods that they
           have used for many years and their traditional way of life/fear of losses;
           They feel that any increased income they generate will be distributed to the family and the community,
           resulting in  little benefit for themselves; fear of distribution of benefits/income with others;
           Many small-scale farmers reject some improved crop varieties due to preferred taste, appearance etc. of the
           traditional crops, even though the yield increases, which can increase their income;
           Traditional / cultural practices;

12   Very few candidates opted for this question.

     (a)   Candidates were able to state the marketing strategy, however, could not explain why it is preferred
           in rural farming.
           Answer
           Co-operative marketing;
           Farmers share the cost/ it reduces the cost of taking their products to the final consumer;
           Shared responsibilities of storage/packaging/distribution transport and marketing;
           Products are marketed together;
           Products are sold directly to the final consumers, which lowers the price;
           Production inputs (such as fertilisers, equipment) are supplied at a lower cost to the members;

     (b)   Well answered as the majority of candidates were able to state the type and the terms for loaning
           capital to farmers.
           Answer
           Short term loans – seasonal loan for production which must be paid back in a period of 1 to 5 years;
           Medium-term loans – for purchasing of equipment and breeding stock and must be paid back in a period of 5
           to 8 years;
           Long-term loans – for purchasing of land and must be paid back in a period of 10 – 25 years;

     (c)   Poorly answered.  Candidates provided general answers that did not address the question directly.
           They could not explain why it is difficult to achieve long-term profitability in perfect competition.
           Answer
           products are the same;
           same price;
           new producers brings the demand curve down/demand becomes low;
           no firm/producer is able to increase the price of produce;

     (d)   This question required candidates to state government policies and tools as well as explain how they
           are used on addition to their effects on the marketing of produce.  Few candidates provided correct answers.
           Answer
           To avoid one group from benefiting at the expense of another group/prevents competition between local and
           imported products;
           To limit the number of goods or the value of goods that a country can import or export during a particular
           period by giving quotas;
           To make the price of commodities affordable to consumers by giving subsidies;
           To help farmers to continue producing for economic growth by supporting certain price levels at or above
           market value;
           To ensure that farmers receive the expected revenue for their products through deficiency payments;

             When referring to the genetic variations, it must be stated very clearly that they are the differences in the
             genetic variation of one species, since different species will always have genetic differences.
             Generally, this question was answered quite well, as most candidates scored marking point 2.

             the number and range of different ecosystems and habitats;
             numbers and diversity of species / the number of species and their relative abundance/ ref. to species
             richness / range of diversity of life / variation of different species in an ecosystem;
             genetic information contained by each member of a species/ the genetic variation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           within each species;

      (c)    To    compare the biodiversity candidates had to compare the diversity index which they had calculated
             to decide which habitat, 1.1 or 1.2 has the greater diversity.
             Very few candidates looked at any other differences between the two habitats which could indicate that there
             are differences between the diversity.
             Some candidates used the word diverse when referring to biodiversity.  Correct terminology should be used.
             Most candidates do not understand that the index must have a value in the range of 0 – 1, some had
             negative answers or an index above 1.

             1.  both ecosystems have a variety of different species / habitat 1: 11 species and habitat 2: 10 species / both
                 ecosystems have large species richness / species diversity (according to Simpson’s index) is (slightly)
                 higher in habitat 1 than 2;
             2.  species evenness greater in habitat 2 than in habitat 1;
             3.  habitat two dominated by Acacia senegal, other species only few, whilst Acacia senegal is less prominent
                 in habitat 1;                    ORA using Boscia albitrunca or Kleina longiflora or ref. to other valid examples
             4.  7 species occur in both habitats;
             5.  4 species occur only in habitat 1, 3 species occur only in habitat 2;

      (d)    Candidates were asked to discuss the need to maintain biodiversity.  Discussion means more than
             just stating a fact.  Just listing facts did not gain the candidates any marks.
             E.g., of common answers given that just stated are: for future generations, for educational purposes, for
             research.
             Teachers are encouraged to elaborate or give further explanations to add value to these answers.
             Candidates also refer to conservation instead of biodiversity.
             Ref. to clearing the land for agricultural purposes does not qualify for a mark, since cutting down the natural
             vegetation to plant crops does not maintain biodiversity.  Candidates write the reason why it is important /
             useful to classify living organisms.
             1.  rich biodiversity speeds up recovery after a natural disaster / allows organisms to adapt to changes;
             2.  rich biodiversity stabilizes the ecosystem and global climate / keeps food chains functional / prevents
                 extinction;
             3.  provides resources for medicinal purposes/ source of food for humans;
             4.  ref. to source of tourism, e.g., helps to attract tourists which generates income for the country;
             5.  preserves culture of people living in the area / enhances recreational activities;
             6.  ref. to ethical reasons/all species have a right to exist / aesthetic value, e.g., for poets / artists;
             7.  provides materials used in industry, e.g., rubber / wood etc. / provides materials for clothes and shelter;
             8.  maintains the gene pool;

Question 2

      (a)    This question asks for a comparison, therefore, facts about both molecules, DNA and RNA must be
             mentioned.  To make the answering of this type of questions easier for the candidates, teach them to
             answer it in a table.
             Some candidates confuse polypeptide with polynucleotide and refer to a single or double structure instead of
             a single strand and a double strand.
             RNA is a ribose sugar while, DNA is a deoxyribose sugar is biologically incorrect and no marks are awarded.
             Many candidates referred to phosphodiester bonds being found in RNA molecules rather than in DNA
             molecules.  It must be made clear that these bonds are found in both molecules.
             Most candidates answered this question quite well.
             1.   RNA contains the sugar ribose, DNA contains the sugar deoxyribose;
             2.   RNA has the nitrogen bases uracil, (adenine, guanine and cytosine) whilst DNA has thymine  instead of
                  uracil;
             3.   RNA is a single stranded molecule, DNA is a double stranded molecule;
             4.   RNA molecules are shorter than DNA molecules;
             5.   In DNA the number of A = T and G = C; in RNA there is no fixed ratio between the bases / DNA has
                  (complimentary) base pairing and RNA does not;  AW
             6.   RNA has a straight chain, DNA in a helix;

